{"name":"Pacific Palisade","hex":"#69a4b9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pacific","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Calm, peaceful.","Preferring peace by nature; avoiding violence."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pacific"},{"term":"palisade","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A long, strong stake, one end of which is set firmly in the ground, and the other sharpened.","A wall of wooden stakes, used as a defensive barrier."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To equip with a palisade."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/palisade"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pacific Ocean","blocks":[{"text":"The Pacific Ocean is the largest and deepest of Earth's five oceanic divisions. It stretches from the Arctic Ocean in the north to the Southern Ocean, or, depending on the definition, to Antarctica in the south, and is bounded by the continents of Asia and Australia in the west and the Americas in the east."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Pacific_Ocean_-_en.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacific_Ocean","wikidata":"Q98"},{"title":"Palisade","blocks":[{"text":"A palisade, sometimes called a stakewall or a paling, is typically a row of closely placed, high vertical standing tree trunks or wooden or iron stakes used as a fence for enclosure or as a defensive wall. Palisades can form a stockade."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/St_Fagans_Celtic_village_palisade.jpg/330px-St_Fagans_Celtic_village_palisade.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palisade","wikidata":"Q90685"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Fijian_double_canoe%2C_model%2C_Otago_Museum%2C_2016-01-29.jpg/500px-Fijian_double_canoe%2C_model%2C_Otago_Museum%2C_2016-01-29.jpg?utm